// @test: verify-fail
// @level: L3
// @tier: 0
// @tags: verification, smt, refinement-reflection, soundness
// @timeout: 30000
// @description: Declaring an untranslatable function's SIGNATURE does
//   not make its BODY known, and is not done at all for a function
//   whose result may vary.
//
// The soundness pole of `opaque_untranslatable_predicate.vr`. That
// spec shows an un-translatable predicate gets a name the solver
// knows; this one bounds what that name buys.
//
// TWO KINDS OF FAILURE LIVE HERE, and telling them apart is the whole
// point of the change:
//
//   §1  claims about the VALUE of an opaque function. The symbol is
//       declared, the goal is well-formed, and the solver correctly
//       answers "I cannot show that". A proof failure on the merits.
//       THREE poles.
//
//   §2  a function that is NOT declared `pure`. No symbol is emitted
//       at all, so the goal is not well-formed. A failure BEFORE the
//       merits. ONE pole, and one CONTROL that passes deliberately.
//
//   §3  prose: the pole that cannot be written, and why its refusal to
//       compile is the answer rather than an obstacle.
//
// FOUR E0319, MEASURED, on both the binary that predates this change
// and the one that carries it. That the number does not MOVE is the
// assertion — a widening change errs by accepting, so its acceptance
// has to contain something that must stay red.
//
// Both report as failure, which is exactly why the distinction had to
// be built rather than observed: before T0905 every reflection decline
// produced the §2 shape, including the ones that deserved §1, so
// "unprovable" and "unmentionable" were one outcome.
//
// A CONTROL THIS FILE CANNOT CARRY ALONE: a parse or type error would
// also read as verify-fail, making every assertion below vacuous. The
// paired pass spec uses the SAME constructs — `pure fn`, `while`, a
// local `mut`, `Int` arithmetic — so a construct-level breakage turns
// that file red. Read the two together; a green pass spec is what
// makes this file's red meaningful.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// §1  DECLARED, AND STILL UNKNOWN
// ---------------------------------------------------------------

/// The same shape as the pass spec's `sum_to`: pure, loop-bodied,
/// declared as an opaque symbol.
public pure fn sum_to(n: Int) -> Int {
    let mut acc: Int = 0;
    let mut i: Int = 0;
    while i < n {
        acc = acc + i;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    acc
}

/// FALSE in general, and — more to the point — not something the
/// declaration could ever support. `sum_to(4)` is 6.
///
/// If this ever proves, the opaque path has started emitting a BODY
/// axiom, which would mean the translator invented a meaning for a
/// loop it declined to read.
@verify(formal)
public theorem an_opaque_symbol_has_no_known_value(n: Int)
    ensures sum_to(n) == 0
    proof by auto;

/// Also false, and it fails for a subtler reason worth pinning: the
/// declaration says `sum_to` is a function, not that it is monotone,
/// non-negative, or bounded. Every property of the BODY is outside
/// what was asserted.
@verify(formal)
public theorem an_opaque_symbol_has_no_known_sign(n: Int)
    ensures sum_to(n) >= 0
    proof by auto;

/// Two different opaque functions are two different symbols. Nothing
/// relates them — in particular, sharing a signature does not.
public pure fn product_to(n: Int) -> Int {
    let mut acc: Int = 1;
    let mut i: Int = 1;
    while i < n {
        acc = acc * i;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    acc
}

@verify(formal)
public theorem two_opaque_symbols_are_unrelated(n: Int)
    ensures sum_to(n) == product_to(n)
    proof by auto;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// §2  NOT DECLARED AT ALL
// ---------------------------------------------------------------

/// The determinism pole. Identical body to `reaches` in the pass spec
/// — the ONLY difference is the missing `pure`.
///
/// Without that modifier nothing warrants asserting the function
/// returns the same answer twice, so no symbol is emitted, and even
/// excluded middle is unavailable. The pass spec proves this exact
/// claim for the `pure` twin; this file failing it is what says the
/// gate is real rather than decorative.
///
/// If this ever proves, the `pure` gate has been dropped, and the
/// opaque path has begun asserting determinism it has no basis for —
/// which is the one way this change could be unsound.
public fn reaches_impure(n: Int) -> Bool {
    let mut i: Int = 0;
    while i < n {
        i = i + 1;
    }
    i >= n
}

@verify(formal)
public theorem an_undeclared_symbol_has_no_excluded_middle(n: Int)
    ensures reaches_impure(n) || !reaches_impure(n)
    proof by auto;

/// NOT A POLE — A CONTROL, and it was written as a pole by mistake.
///
/// The intent was "say the same thing as determinism directly, so the
/// failure cannot be blamed on the shape of the disjunction". It
/// PASSES, on both binaries, and it should: the goal translator renders
/// a call as `(name args…)` keyed on the name, so both sides are the
/// same TERM and congruence closure closes it whether or not any symbol
/// was declared.
///
/// The pass spec's `term_identity_needs_no_declaration` is the same
/// observation about a DECLARED symbol. Together they are the useful
/// half: `f(x) == f(x)` proves for a declared symbol AND for an
/// undeclared one, so it distinguishes nothing and is evidence for
/// nothing. Excluded middle, one theorem above, is the claim that
/// actually needs the declaration — and it is the one that moved.
///
/// Kept in the file so the next reader finds the note instead of
/// writing the pole again. Twice in one pair was enough.
@verify(formal)
public theorem term_identity_proves_for_an_undeclared_symbol_too(n: Int)
    ensures reaches_impure(n) == reaches_impure(n)
    proof by auto;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// §3  THE POLE THAT CANNOT BE WRITTEN, AND WHY THAT IS THE ANSWER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This section held a third pole: a function declared `pure` that
// mutates a module static, whose excluded middle must still fail. The
// reasoning was that §2's gate rests on the author's word and
// `two_calls_to_one_impure_function_are_not_equal.vr` (L0, red until
// T0982) says the property inferrer does not treat a `static mut` write
// as state at all — so the word could be false and be believed.
//
// THE POLE DOES NOT COMPILE, and the refusal is the finding. Measured
// on the same binary as every number in this file:
//
//     error<E503>: pure function `ticking` has side effects:
//                  Mutates, WritesExternal, ReadsExternal
//     help: remove the `pure` modifier or eliminate side effects
//
// So `pure` IS enforced against a static write, and T0982's sentence
// is about a DIFFERENT gate: its `tick()` is declared plain `fn`, and
// the defect is what the inferrer COMPUTES for an undeclared function,
// not what E503 checks on a declared one. Two gates, one word, and
// reading the first as evidence about the second is how a pole that
// could not exist got written.
//
// Measured alongside it, three files, one pole each, because a refusal
// fails the whole compilation unit and poles sharing one cannot be told
// apart:
//
//     `pure` + print()                -> REFUSED  E503
//     `pure` + a `using [...]` call   -> REFUSED  E018
//     genuinely pure                  -> ACCEPTED 0 errors
//
// Two refusals and one acceptance: the modifier is real, so §2's gate
// is real, and the opaque path rests on something the compiler checks
// rather than on an author's promise.
//
// Left as prose rather than deleted, because "there is no third pole"
// and "the third pole was never tried" look identical in a spec file,
// and only one of them is a measurement.

fn main() {
    print("opaque-untranslatable-unsound");
}
